WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.859
Your first days in weightlessness.

2
00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:06.146
Here's what your body goes through, and
how to get past it.

3
00:00:07.396 --> 00:00:11.862
Day one. Lucas has just arrived, and the
whole room seems to spin.

4
00:00:12.762 --> 00:00:15.419
His inner ear can't find up or down
anymore,

5
00:00:15.419 --> 00:00:18.136
but his eyes still see a floor and a
ceiling.

6
00:00:18.176 --> 00:00:21.829
The two signals don't match, and his brain
gets confused.

7
00:00:22.729 --> 00:00:26.063
That's space motion sickness. Most
newcomers feel it,

8
00:00:26.063 --> 00:00:28.957
and it usually fades within two to three
days.

9
00:00:29.857 --> 00:00:32.775
At the same time, about two liters of
fluid shift

10
00:00:32.775 --> 00:00:34.860
from your legs up toward your head.

11
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.941
Your face gets puffy, your nose feels
blocked, and your legs get thinner.

12
00:00:39.981 --> 00:00:43.625
Astronauts call it puffy face, bird legs.
It's normal.

13
00:00:44.525 --> 00:00:46.580
What helps: move your head slowly,

14
00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:50.872
keep the same up as your crewmates, eat
light, and drink in small sips.

15
00:00:51.772 --> 00:00:54.756
There's also medicine for it in the
medical kit.

16
00:00:54.796 --> 00:00:58.992
If you keep vomiting, or can't keep water
down, tell Keiko right away.

17
00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:04.361
Within a few days, your body adapts, and
floating starts to feel like home.

